18/19 @logic scenarios pass! The "Save edit on blur" scenario fails because "Buy oat milk" isn't found. The issue is in my blur handling - the `setTimeout` in the dblclick handler delays the blur event listener, so the blur might fire before the listener is attached. Let me look at the scenario:

```
Given I have a todo "Buy milk"
When I edit "Buy milk" to "Buy oat milk" and blur the input
Then the todo list shows "Buy oat milk"
And the todo "Buy milk" should not exist
```

The step definition for "I edit {string} to {string} and blur the input" does:
1. Double-click to activate edit mode
2. Set the edit input value
3. Dispatch blur event

But the app's implementation wraps the blur listener in `setTimeout`. So when I dispatch the blur event, the listener might not be attached yet. Let me fix the step definition to wait a tick before blurring.

Or better: I should fix the app code. The `setTimeout` approach is fragile. Let me use the `setTimeout(0)` approach but add the blur listener synchronously too.

Actually, looking at my app code more carefully:

```javascript
var finishEdit = function (save) {
  var val = editInput.value.trim();
  if (save && val) {
    todoRef.title = val;
    saveTodos();
  } else if (save && !val) {
    var idx = todos.indexOf(todoRef);
    if (idx !== -1) todos.splice(idx, 1);
    saveTodos();
  }
  item.classList.remove('editing');
  render();
};

function onKeydown(e) {
  if (e.keyCode === ENTER_KEY) { ... finishEdit(true); }
  else if (e.keyCode === ESCAPE_KEY) { ... item.classList.remove('editing'); }
}

function onBlur() {
  editInput.removeEventListener('keydown', onKeydown);
  editInput.removeEventListener('blur', onBlur);
  finishEdit(true);  // <-- saves on blur
}

editInput.addEventListener('keydown', onKeydown);
setTimeout(function () { editInput.addEventListener('blur', onBlur); }, 0);
```

The issue is the blur event fires during the same microtask/macrotask as the setTimeout(0) callback. With happy-dom, setTimeout(0) might run before the event dispatch, or after. The race condition causes the blur to fire when there's no listener.

I should fix this in the step definitions by adding a small delay before dispatching blur. Or I should fix the app to not use setTimeout for attaching the blur listener.

Actually, the simplest fix: in the step definition, I'll add the blur listener synchronously and then dispatch the blur event. But that won't work because I need the app to have attached its listener first.

Let me fix it in the step definition - add a small delay or tick before dispatching blur.

